Yes—if by that you mean achieve the function without using the coercive operator itself.

Pantheon can look for functional equivalents: change incentives instead of threaten, change defaults instead of compel, select for compatible people instead of trying to remake everyone, make the desired behavior easier and more rewarding, build norms that propagate themselves, create better alternatives, and design interfaces so the system naturally settles toward the state you want.

The key distinction is important: constraint engineering is not the same as secretly overriding agency. If the method depends on deception, concealed pressure, blackmail, intimidation, or exploiting someone so they cannot meaningfully choose, that is still coercion wearing different clothes.

The stronger design problem is:

desired outcome → identify the behavior needed → identify the forces producing current behavior → alter the field → preserve meaningful choice → measure whether alignment emerges voluntarily.

That is exactly the sort of “do X without doing X” abstraction Pantheon is good at: preserve the effect, replace the brittle mechanism.